nation System (NPDES)\nL147: \nL148: * * *\nL149: ## Overview\nL150: The Water Boards develop and issue general permits to cover multiple facilities within a specific category. The use of general permits allows the Water Boards to allocate resources in a more efficient manner and provide timely permit coverage for large numbers of facilities in the same category. In addition, the use of a general permit ensures consistency of permit conditions for similar facilities.\nL151: According to cite95†40 CFR section 122.28†www.gpo.gov , general permits may be written to cover categories of point sources having common elements, such as:\nL152:   * Storm water point sources (\"Storm Water\" is a separate program at the State Water Board) - see cite41†Stormwater program page L153:   * Facilities that involve the same or substantially similar types of operations\nL154:   * Facilities that discharge the same types of wastewater\nL155:   * Facilities that require the same effluent limitations or operating conditions\nL156:   * Facilities that require the same monitoring where tiered conditions may be used for minor differences within class (e.g., size or seasonal activity)\nL157:   * Facilities that are more appropriately regulated by a general permit\nL158: However, general permits may only be issued to dischargers within a specific geographical area such as the following:\nL159: \nL160:   * City, County, or State boundary\nL161:   * Designated planning area\nL162:   * Sewer district\nL163:   * Standard metropolitan statistical area\nL164:   * State highway system\nL165:   * Urbanized area\nL166: ## Additional Information\nL167: \nL168:   * cite96†Ex Parte Disclosure Requirements for Pending General Orders L169:   * cite97†Fees L170: ## Water Board General NPDES Wastewater Permits\nL171: \nL172: Below is a table that provides a list of NPDES Wastewater General Permits throughout California. It also provides a link to the program page or permit itself.\nL173: \nL174: 